  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The three correct actions you can perform with CloudWatch Logs natively are:</p><ul><li><b>Transmit the logs to Amazon Kinesis for additional downstream processing.</b> CloudWatch Logs supports streaming data to Amazon Kinesis Streams or Kinesis Data Firehose, enabling you to trigger advanced analytics or custom data workflows downstream.</li><li><b>Direct log events to an AWS Lambda function for customized processing.</b> You can create CloudWatch Logs subscriptions that invoke Lambda functions, which is frequently used for real-time custom validation, transformation, or alerting of log data as soon as it is received.</li><li><b>Stream log data to Amazon Elasticsearch (now known as OpenSearch) for advanced search and analysis.</b> CloudWatch Logs allows you to send logs directly to Elasticsearch, which lets you search, visualize, and analyze log data efficiently.</li></ul> <p>The option <i>Send log data to SQS queues for further actions.</i> is <b>incorrect</b> because CloudWatch Logs does not natively support sending log data to SQS (Simple Queue Service) as a destination. Any such integration would require intermediate processing, such as using Lambda to manually push log events to SQS, which is not an out-of-the-box feature.</p> <p><b>Tip:</b> For log-based integrations and searching, prefer services like Kinesis, Lambda, and Elasticsearch, all of which have direct subscription or streaming options from CloudWatch Logs. Familiarize yourself with the normal flow options for CloudWatch Logs and their intended use cases for AWS monitoring and alerting architectures.</p>",
